== Four-cylinder ==

===Petrol===

====<span class="anchor" id="GH10"></span><span class="anchor" id="GH12"></span><span class="anchor" id="GH100"></span>GH engine====
Isuzu's first petrol engines were license built Hillman units for the locally assembled [[Hillman Minx|Minx]], from 1953. Called the '''GH10''' it has a bore of {{convert|65|mm|in|2|abbr=on}} and a stroke of {{convert|95|mm|in|2|abbr=on}} for a displacement of {{convert|1260|cc|cuin|1|abbr=on}}. Power is {{convert|37.5|PS|kW|0|abbr=on}}. In 1955 this was updated to the '''GH12''', a square design with a 76.2&nbsp;mm bore and stroke for a displacement of {{convert|1390|cc|cuin|1|abbr=on}}. This was upgraded in 1956 for more power, {{convert|46|PS|kW|0|abbr=on}} rather than the original's {{convert|43|PS|kW|0|abbr=on}}, and was renamed '''GH100'''. In 1958, power increased yet again, to {{convert|50|PS|kW|0|abbr=on}}.<ref name="Trucks18-72">{{Cite book | last = Ozeki | first = Kazuo | ref = 1918-72 |script-title=ja:日本のトラック・バス 1918~1972 |trans-title=Japanese Trucks and Buses 1918-1972 | publisher = Miki Press | year = 2007 | page = 54 | location = Tokyo | language = Japanese | isbn = 978-4-89522-494-9 }}</ref>

====<span class="anchor" id="GL150"></span>GL engine====
For 1959 Isuzu developed their own square design ({{convert|78|mm|in|2|abbr=on|disp=or}}) called the '''GL150''', Isuzu's first own petrol engine.<ref>{{cite web | script-title = ja:ものづくりの歴史 いすゞ自動車株式会社 | trans-title = Manufacturing history of Isuzu Motors | url = http://www.recruit.isuzu.co.jp/shinsotsu/history/index.html | publisher = Isuzu Motors | accessdate = 2014-01-08 | work = Isuzu Recruiting Information | language = Japanese | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20140108123058/http://www.recruit.isuzu.co.jp/shinsotsu/history/index.html | archive-date = 2014-01-08 | url-status = dead }}</ref> Still showing unmistakable Hillman origins it displaces {{convert|1491|cc|cuin|1|abbr=on}} and has {{convert|60|PS|kW|0|abbr=on}}.<ref name="Trucks18-72"/> The GL150 was fitted to the 1959 [[Isuzu Elf]] and the 1961 [[Isuzu Bellel|Bellel]].

====General Motors====

* The [[Isuzu Hombre]] used a General Motors-built [[GM Vortec engine#2200|Vortec 2200 Engine]] with 118&nbsp;hp (86&nbsp;kW) and 140&nbsp;ft·lb (190 N·m) of torque.
* 2004–2006 [[Isuzu i-Series]] used a General Motors-built [[GM Atlas engine#LK5|Vortec 2800 Engine]] with 175&nbsp;hp (130&nbsp;kW) and 185&nbsp;ft·lb (251 N·m) of torque.
* 2007–2008 [[Isuzu i-Series]] use a General Motors-built [[GM Atlas engine#LLV|Vortec 2900 Engine]] with 185&nbsp;hp (138&nbsp;kW) and 195&nbsp;ft·lb (263 N·m) of torque.

